    "final_answer": "I now have a comprehensive understanding of the article. Let me provide the summary of the latest health-related news article on BBC News.\n\n## Summary of the Latest Health-Related News Article on BBC News\n\n**Article Title:** \"'This is fixable': What really helps save a relationship\"\n**Author:** Sophie Hardach\n**Published:** 35 minutes ago (this is the most recent article on the BBC Health page)\n\n### Key Points Discussed:\n\n**1. Health Impacts of Relationship Quality**\n- Vicious arguments can result in poor sleep, heart pain, and muscle pain\n- Relationship conflict can make everyone in the family — including children — more vulnerable to physical and mental health problems\n- A happy relationship can boost health and even lifespan\n- An unhappy relationship can have the opposite effect, raising the risk of earlier death\n- Poor-quality close relationships are considered a health risk factor equal to or greater than smoking or lack of exercise\n\n**2. What Couples Argue About**\n- Couples still argue about the same types of issues 69% of the time (per psychologist John Gottman's longitudinal study)\n- Common causes include disagreements over money and unequal division of housework\n- Both happy and unhappy couples argue — the crucial difference is *how* they behave towards each other\n\n**3. Four Behaviors That Can 'Kill a Relationship' (Gottman's \"Four Horsemen\")**\n- **Criticism**\n- **Contempt**\n- **Defensiveness**\n- **Stonewalling** (withdrawal from conflict)\n- Notably, persistent criticism from a partner significantly predicts an earlier death\n\n**4. What Helps Build a Healthy Relationship**\n- **Responsiveness** — feeling that your partner listens, supports, and understands you — is linked to greater marital satisfaction, can buffer inflammation, and boost physical health and longevity\n- **Kindness and cooperation** build long-term goodwill\n- A 20-year study found shared decision-making and equally shared housework were associated with greater marital happiness and lower conflict\n- **Constructive arguments** are associated with good sleep, while destructive ones are linked to poor sleep\n\n**5. The Role of Therapy in Rebuilding Empathy**\n- Couples therapy has a positive effect on about 70% of couples who do it\n- It can improve relationship issues, boost satisfaction, prevent relapse, and strengthen emotional intimacy\n- It can also help improve individual emotional or physical health problems\n- Empathy is often \"eaten away\" in struggling couples, but therapy can help rebuild it\n- Working on the relationship is about \"being aware of what you have and making it work\"\n\nThe article's central message is that while unhappy relationships pose serious health risks — comparable to smoking or inactivity — most relationship problems are fixable through better communication, mutual responsiveness, and professional support when needed.",
